  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "P.R.Santhosh Kumar vs Food Inspector on 3 April, 2019 In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr. Justice R. Narayana Pisharadi Wednesday,The 03Rd Day Of April 2019 / 13Th Chaithra, 1941 Crl.MC.No. 2246 of 2016 Cc 542/2015 of Additional Chief Judicial Magistrate,Thalassery Petitioner/Claim Petitioner: P.R.Santhosh Kumar Aged 44 Years, S/O. P.K.Radhakrishnan, Narishanthara Parambil, Perandoor Road, Elamakkara, Kochi-682 026. By Adv. Sri.Millu Dandapani Respondent/Complainant: Food Inspector Mobile Vigilance Squad, Kozhikode-673 001, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam Cochi - 682 031.",
    "facts": "The petitioner is the third accused in the case C.C.No.542/2015 pending before the Court of the Additional Chief Judicial Magistrate, Thalassery. The petition is filed by him to quash the proceedings against him in the aforesaid case. 2. On 23.03.1995, the Food Inspector, Mobile Vigilance Squad, Kozhikode inspected the shop by name 'Shalimar Agencies' at Thalassery. He purchased three bottles of lime pickle from the shop and took them as samples. One of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst, Pondicherry. The report of analysis received from the Public Analyst, Pondicherry showed that the sample of lime pickle was adulterated. On the basis of the aforesaid report, the Food Inspector filed complaint in the court concerned under Sections 16(1)(a)(i) and 16(1)(a)(ii) read with Section 2(ia)(m) and Section 7(i) and 7(iii) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (hereinafter referred to as 'the Act'). The aforesaid case is pending before the Court of the Additional Chief Judicial Magistrate, Thalassery as C.C.No.542/2015. The first accused in the case is the salesman of the shop and the second accused is the licencee of the shop. The petitioner, who is the third accused in the case, is the manager and nominee of the manufacturer of the alleged adulterated lime pickle.",
    "arguments": "3. Heard learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor. 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner contended that the analysis of the food item was conducted in a laboratory which was not notified as per any rule made under the Act and the analysis was made not as per any validated method as per the rules and therefore, the court could not have taken cognizance of the offences alleged against the petitioner. Learned counsel relied upon the decision of the Honourable Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings Pvt. Ltd. v. Food Inspector : (2011) 1 SCC 176 in support of his contention. Section 7(iii) of the Act states that no person shall himself or by any person on his behalf manufacture for sale, or store, sell or distribute any article of food for the sale of which a licence is prescribed, except in accordance with the conditions of the licence. In Pepsico (supra), the Apex Court has held that the provisions of Sections 23(1A)(ee) and (hh) of the Act are mandatory and that the non-formulation of Rules under the aforesaid provisions of the Act would be fatal for the prosecution. 7. The dictum in Pepsico (supra) has been clarified by a Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector : 2016 (2) KLT 390 and it has been held that the aforesaid decision of the Apex Court would apply to all cases of food adulteration under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act where the prosecution is launched on the basis of the report of a public analyst. It has also been held that in order to be reliable and to be taken as the basis for the purpose of launching prosecution, a report by a public analyst must be one made after conducting an analysis in a laboratory defined under Section 23(1A)(ee) of the Act.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Heard learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor. 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner contended that the analysis of the food item was conducted in a laboratory which was not notified as per any rule made under the Act and the analysis was made not as per any validated method as per the rules and therefore, the court could not have taken cognizance of the offences alleged against the petitioner. Learned counsel relied upon the decision of the Honourable Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings Pvt. Ltd. v. Food Inspector : (2011) 1 SCC 176 in support of his contention.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Section 7(iii) of the Act states that no person shall himself or by any person on his behalf manufacture for sale, or store, sell or distribute any article of food for the sale of which a licence is prescribed, except in accordance with the conditions of the licence. In Pepsico (supra), the Apex Court has held that the provisions of Sections 23(1A)(ee) and (hh) of the Act are mandatory and that the non-formulation of Rules under the aforesaid provisions of the Act would be fatal for the prosecution. 7. The dictum in Pepsico (supra) has been clarified by a Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector : 2016 (2) KLT 390 and it has been held that the aforesaid decision of the Apex Court would apply to all cases of food adulteration under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act where the prosecution is launched on the basis of the report of a public analyst. It has also been held that in order to be reliable and to be taken as the basis for the purpose of launching prosecution, a report by a public analyst must be one made after conducting an analysis in a laboratory defined under Section 23(1A)(ee) of the Act."
  },
